{"answer":"The critic's case conflates two distinct levels of the target's design and misattributes a contradiction that does not exist.\n\n1) 'SRC_001 changes the semantic label… from a Boolean to a finite proof-object certificate, contradicting the requirement to preserve the existing label set of the fixed point.'\n\nNot established. The target explicitly states it does 'not alter the fixed point's label set.' The semantic label attached to a suspended node—Boolean OUT versus a finite set of defeating arguments—is a representational annotation on that node, not a change to the label algebra or to the iterative operator whose least fixed point is being computed. Preserving 'the existing label set' of the fixed point means preserving the space of labels and the operator that generates the fixed point, which the target keeps intact. Nothing in the problem statement forbids adding a certificate structure alongside an unchanged label, and the target affirms the fixed point's label set is unchanged. The critic has not shown that this certificate is a member of the fixed point's label set or that its presence redefines the operator.\n\n2) 'The target's own description says validity becomes a property of certificates rather than of committed roots, contradicting the problem's guarantee on the validity of every committed root.'\n\nNot established as a contradiction. The phrase 'validity becomes a property of certificates rather than of committed roots' describes where the burden of establishing validity is discharged: in the proof-search over the certificate set accompanying a suspended node. It is a statement about the operational route by which validity is checked, not a denial that committed roots are valid. The problem's guarantee concerns the semantic status of committed roots, which is preserved because the fixed-point construction and label semantics are not altered. The target strengthens the epistemic status of suspension by replacing a Boolean tag with the defeating arguments that justify it; it does not say committed roots lose validity. At most, it imposes a new proof-theoretic obligation on reconsideration of suspended nodes, which is a computational/proof-search condition, not a change to the truth of already-committed roots.\n\n3) 'It imposes a proof-theoretic obligation that may not be decidable in polynomial time for arbitrary attacker sets.'\n\nThis is a genuine limitation disclosed by the target itself, but it does not establish the alleged contradiction with the label set or the validity guarantee. The undecidability or super-polynomial cost of certificate proof-search over arbitrary attacker sets affects the cost of reconsidering suspended nodes; it does not alter the fixed point, its label set, or the validity of committed roots. The critic has not shown that the problem requires reconsideration to be polynomial, nor that the guarantee on committed roots entails any complexity bound on proof-search over certificates.\n\nConceded only: the target acknowledges a potential non-polynomial proof-search obligation. Denied: that this changes the fixed point's label set or that it contradicts the guarantee of valid committed roots. The target explicitly preserves the fixed point and its label set while reframing only the evidential basis on which suspension is reconsidered."}